      "text": "To solve for the two positive real numbers whose geometric mean is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and harmonic mean is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\), we will use the definitions of the geometric mean and harmonic mean.\n\nLet the two numbers be \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\n1. **Geometric Mean:**\n\n   The geometric mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Squaring both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   ab = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Harmonic Mean:**\n\n   The harmonic mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2ab}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(ab = 3\\) into the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{6}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Cross-multiplying to solve for \\(a + b\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   6 \\cdot 2 = 3 \\cdot (a + b)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 = 3(a + b)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + b = 4\n   \\]\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n\\[\nab = 3\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nThese equations define a quadratic equation whose roots are \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). The quadratic equation can be written as:\n\\[\nt^2 - (a + b)t + ab = 0\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\nt^2 - 4t + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(t = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 1\\), \\(B = -4\\), and \\(C = 3\\):\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\(\\boxed{1, 3}\\).",
