      "text": "To solve the problem of finding two positive real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) given their geometric mean \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and harmonic mean \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\), we will use the definitions of geometric mean and harmonic mean.\n\nThe geometric mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nab = 3\n\\]\n\nThe harmonic mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{2ab}{a+b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(ab = 3\\) into the harmonic mean formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{a+b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{a+b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to solve for \\(a+b\\), we get:\n\\[\n6 \\cdot 2 = 3 \\cdot (a+b)\n\\]\n\\[\n12 = 3(a+b)\n\\]\n\\[\na+b = 4\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n1. \\(ab = 3\\)\n2. \\(a + b = 4\\)\n\nThese equations suggest that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nt^2 - (a+b)t + ab = 0\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we found:\n\\[\nt^2 - 4t + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{4 + 2}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = \\frac{4 - 2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the two numbers are \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 1\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3, 1}\n\\]",
