      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two positive real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that the geometric mean and the harmonic mean are given as \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\) respectively.\n\nThe geometric mean of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nab = 3 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\nThe harmonic mean of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{2ab}{a+b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nFrom this, we can rearrange to get:\n\\[\n4ab = 3(a + b) \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(ab = 3\\) from equation (1) into equation (2), we get:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 3 = 3(a + b)\n\\]\n\\[\n12 = 3(a + b)\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = 4 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of quadratic equations:\n1. \\(ab = 3\\)\n2. \\(a + b = 4\\)\n\nWe can solve this system by treating \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) as the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - (a+b)x + ab = 0\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a + b = 4\\) and \\(ab = 3\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 + 2}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 1\\) (or vice versa). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3, 1}\n\\]",
